.na
.nh
.TH multifiltsetup 1 4.10.11 IMOD
.SH NAME
multifiltsetup \- Test multiple reconstruction filter values on a subvolume
.SH SYNOPSIS
multifiltsetup  options  tilt_com_file_name
.SH DESCRIPTION
Multifiltsetup produces command files for reconstructing a defined
region of a tomogram with a series of values for one of the types of
radial filters in Tilt(1).  Four kinds of filters can be varied, but
only one kind can be changed on any given run.
.P
Two filters providing an alternative form for the ramp function, the
SIRT-like filter and "exact filters", can be varied with:
  \fB-fake\fR    \fIList of iteration values\fR
  \fB-exact\fR   \fIList of "object sizes"\fR
.br
The two alternative high-frequency filtering options, the standard
Gaussian filter and the Hamming-like filter, can be varied with:
  \fB-cutoff\fR   \fIList of cutoffs\fR and/or \fB-falloff\fR  \fIList of falloffs\fR
  \fB-hamming\fR  \fIList of start frequencies\fR
.P
The behavior and requirements when varying the Gaussian filter are
complicated because there are two parameters.  The rules are these:
  1) If both \fB-cutoff\fR and \fB-falloff\fR are entered, either both
must have the same number of frequencies listed, or one must
have only a single value.
  2) If \fB-falloff\fR is entered but not \fB-cutoff\fR, a fixed cutoff
for all runs is taken from the "RADIAL" entry in the input command file.
  3) If \fB-falloff\fR is entered with multiple values and
\fB-cutoff\fR with one value, that cutoff is used for all of the runs
  4) If \fB-cutoff\fR is entered but not \fB-falloff\fR, a fixed ratio of
falloff to cutoff is taken from the ratio of the falloff and cutoff
values of the "RADIAL" entry in the input command file.
  5) If \fB-cutoff\fR is entered with multiple values and
\fB-falloff\fR with one value, a fixed ratio of falloff to cutoff is
taken from the ratio of the entered falloff value and the first cutoff.
  6) If \fB-cutoff\fR is entered with multiple values and a falloff is
not entered for each, then the falloff values will all be this fixed
ratio times their respective cutoff values.
.br
This scaling will not work if the first cutoff is 0, or if the falloff
is 0.
.P
For example, if \fB-cutoff\fR is entered as "0.1,0.2,0.3", no
\fB-falloff\fR is entered, and the "RADIAL" entry in the file is
"0.35 0.035", then the ratio will be 0.035 / 0.35 = 0.1 and the
falloffs will be 0.01, 0.02, and 0.03.  If \fB-falloff\fR is entered
with just "0.02", the ratio will be 0.02 / 0.1 = 0.2 and the falloffs
will be 0.02, 0.04, and 0.06.
.P
Unless options are entered to specify a subvolume, the default is to
reconstruction the full area with the same thickness.
.SH OPTIONS
Multifiltsetup uses the PIP package for input (see the manual page
for pip(1)).  Options can be specified either as command line
arguments (with the -) or one per line in a command file
(without the -).  Options can be abbreviated to unique letters; the currently
valid abbreviations for short names are shown in parentheses.
INSERT OPTION TEXT HERE
.TP
.B -StandardInput
Read parameter entries from standard input

.SH FILES
The command files are named "tilt_mulfil-*.com" for an input file
"tilt.com", "tilt_mulfila-*.com" for "tilta.com", etc.  For \fB-fake\fR and
\fB-exact\fR options, they produce tomograms "setname_slfinnn.mrc" and
"setname_efosnnn.mrc", where "setname" is the root name of the data set
and "nnn" is the iteration number or object size,
respectively, and all files in a run will be 2, 3, or 4 digits with
leading zeros as needed.  For the \fB-hamming\fR option, the
tomograms are "setname_hlfs0.xxx.mrc" where "0.xxx" is the starting frequency to 3
decimal places.  When varying the Gaussian filter, the tomogram names
contain both the cutoff and falloff as "setname_gfc0.xxx-f0.xxx.mrc".
.SH AUTHOR
.nf
David Mastronarde
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
tilt(1), proceschunks(1)
.SH BUGS
